   "gap": "Supply complete proof routes, with exact hypotheses, for the Barwise ch. A.1 claims that the handbook records as missing work: (1) p.44 failure of Beth's theorem for weak second-order logic: steps (b) an L*-sentence in a new unary T stating Tarski's truth clauses for codes of first-order sentences with TA as unique solution over N, with correctness of the L*-formulas for the syntactic relations and the induction showing uniqueness, and (c) a coding of HF(omega) in N making every relation on omega definable in HF(N) definable in N, with the translation of L*-formulas into L_A-formulas and its correctness (owned body 616-642); (2) p.44 L(Q_0) essentially equivalent to omega-logic and weak second-order logic: a precise notion and, for each pair of logics, a translation with its proof (owned body 643-652); (3) p.16 decidability of the theory of abelian groups (effective reduction to invariant sentences and its truth preservation) and undecidability of the theory of groups (an interpretation of an essentially undecidable theory and the transfer) (owned body 800-809); (4) the inputs of thm:p02-w02c-p02-003-acf and thm:p02-w02c-p02-003-integers: the Steinitz classification behind thm:p03-acfp-uncountable-categorical (cited), the fundamental theorem of algebra, existence of an algebraically closed field of characteristic 0, Lagrange's four-square theorem (cited to Davis), and a particular coding with the definability property assumed in part 2 (owned body 810-819); (5) for thm:p02-w02c-p02-003-integers-second: derivability conditions D2 and D3 for the provability formula of T-sharp, the representability theorem it rests on (thm:representability is a cited theorem), and a Turing-machine enumeration in place of the Church-Turing thesis (owned body 820-829).",
   "blocker": {
    "kind": "blocked_source",
    "ref": "Keisler1970 (L(Q) completeness/compactness, not held); Barwise1974Axioms ACQ-2026-10-0032 (exact hypotheses of 5.6-5.7, not held)"
